How Tuff combines this Library
------------------------------

electron-vite compiles these modules into Tuff's Electron main-process,
preload and renderer bundles. They are not shipped as a separately
replaceable shared library, so Tuff does not rely on LGPL section 4(d)(1).

Under LGPL section 4(d)(0), every packaged Tuff build ships the following in
its resources directory under legal/pi-desktop-reuse/:

  LICENSE                             GNU LGPL v3 text
  COPYING                             GNU GPL v3 text
  NOTICE                              this file
  BUILDING.md                         rebuild and recombination instructions
  sources.json                        file-level upstream mapping
  corresponding-source.tar.gz         Minimal Corresponding Source of this
                                      Library plus the Corresponding
                                      Application Code it was built with
  corresponding-source.tar.gz.sha256  SHA-256 of the archive
  source-manifest.json                every archived file with its SHA-256,
                                      size and build role, the importers of
                                      each Library file, and digests of the
                                      main/preload/renderer outputs it matches

The archive is produced from the modules the build actually loaded. It is
identified by its SHA-256, not by a Git URL. Tuff's application code inside
the archive stays under the license its package declares: the archived root
LICENSE is MPL-2.0, and some workspace packages (for example tuffex) declare
MIT in their archived package.json or LICENSE. Both permit modification and
recombination.
BUILDING.md explains how to rebuild the bundles with a modified version of
this Library and run the result without any release signing credentials.
